PLACEHOLDER — NO LICENCE HAS BEEN CHOSEN YET. THIS IS A DECISION FOR THE OWNER.

Until this file is replaced with a real licence, the default applies:
ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Nobody may copy, modify or redistribute this work.

Publishing a repository without a licence does NOT make it open source. A
public repository with no licence is "all rights reserved" by default: readers
may view it, but they have no legal right to use, fork, modify or redistribute
any of it. That may be exactly what you intend — plenty of work is published to
be read and not to be reused — but it should be a decision, not an oversight.
State it either way, because silence reads as an oversight.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THIS REPOSITORY CONTAINS, WHICH IS WHY ONE LICENCE MAY NOT FIT ALL OF IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Three quite different kinds of work live here:

  1. SOFTWARE — several hundred Python modules that build geometry, materials,
     simulation and audio, plus shell tooling and gates.

  2. A CREATIVE WORK — the film itself, and the artistic decisions embodied in
     the beat sheet, camera path, grade and audio design. The rendered master
     is not in this repository, but the recipe that produces it is.

  3. A WRITTEN RECORD — docs/, above all docs/DEFECT-LOG-R2.md: ~62,000 lines
     of engineering narrative that is arguably the most valuable artefact here
     and is prose, not code.

It is normal and reasonable to license these differently — for example, code
under a software licence and documentation plus film under a Creative Commons
licence. If you do that, say so explicitly here and name which paths each
licence covers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A CONSTRAINT THAT MUST BE RESOLVED BEFORE CHOOSING: BLENDER AND THE GPL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is not a formality; it is the one genuine legal constraint on this
repository, and it points away from a permissive licence for the code.

Blender is GPL-licensed. The Blender Foundation's stated position is that
Python scripts which use the `bpy` API and are distributed publicly are treated
as derivative works of Blender and must be released under a GPL-compatible
licence. Nearly all the code here is exactly that: modules executed inside
Blender through `bpy`.

Consequences to weigh:

  * Licensing this code MIT or BSD would, on the Blender Foundation's reading,
    be inconsistent with that position. GPL-3.0-or-later (or GPL-2.0-or-later,
    matching Blender itself) is the conservative choice.

  * The film's RENDERED OUTPUT is not affected. Blender's licence explicitly
    does not claim the images you produce with it; those are yours, and you may
    license them however you like — including all rights reserved.

  * The documentation is not `bpy` code and is not constrained by this at all.

If any of this matters commercially, get an actual legal opinion. Nothing here
is legal advice.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RECOMMENDATION, TO BE ACCEPTED OR REJECTED BY THE OWNER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A defensible default, if you want the work to be readable and reusable:

    code (world/, tools/, sim/, audio/, render/, anim/, telemetry/)
        GPL-3.0-or-later          — consistent with the bpy constraint above

    documentation (docs/, README.md, watch/)
        CC BY-SA 4.0              — attribution preserved on the written record

    the film and any rendered frames
        CC BY-NC-ND 4.0, or all rights reserved — your call; this is the
        artistic deliverable and the most likely thing to be reused without
        credit

If instead you want this published to be READ but not reused, replace this file
with a short, explicit notice saying so. That is a perfectly legitimate choice
and it is much better stated than left implied.

DO NOT PUBLISH WITH THIS PLACEHOLDER STILL IN PLACE without at least deciding
that "all rights reserved" is what you want.
